WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.820
A, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 45 from May 21st, 2026.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:10.600
Let's break down today's coolest AI news, so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:15.200
Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:16.400
Let's get into it.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.780
So imagine you're texting a friend late at night about something that's really bothering

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:21.100
you.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:25.940
Instead of a generic reply, you get thoughtful answers that feel safe and careful.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.540
That is the idea behind a new AI called the Path.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.940
It was built by people who used to work at Calm and with Tony Robbins.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.500
Think of it like a super careful tutor who knows exactly when to suggest real help instead

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:40.280
of just guessing.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:47.020
The team trained their model to score 95 out of 100 on a special mental health safety test

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:53.000
called Vera MH, regular chatbots usually top out around 65 on the same test.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.940
The model checks its own responses against strict safety rules before answering.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.180
This stops it from giving dangerous advice about things like self-harm or crisis situations.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.500
Here's why that matters for you.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.820
Lots of teens and students already turned to AI chatbots when they feel stressed or anxious.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.040
Those tools were never built for emotional support in the first place.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.420
A safer version could mean better homework help when you were overwhelmed.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.700
It could also give you a place to sort through feelings without worrying the AI will say something

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:25.180
harmful.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.200
This opens doors for future careers and building responsible AI tools that actually help people.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.700
Right now, the project is still in early stages.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.580
Keep an eye on their updates if this interests you.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:43.280
Remember that any AI chat about serious feelings should always be paired with talking to a real

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.640
person or professional when things feel heavy.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.720
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive, where we look under the hood.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:56.540
You know how when you are baking cookies and you keep reusing the same batch of dough over and over.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.520
The cookies start tasting weird and falling apart because there are no fresh ingredients left.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:08.520
That is basically what happens to some AI models when they run out of new human written text to learn from.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.640
Researchers found that if an AI only trains on stuff other

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.400
AI's have already made up, it starts repeating the same patterns.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:20.140
It makes more mistakes and gets worse at answering questions.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.700
The fix they are testing is simple.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.780
Keep mixing in real fresh writing that actual people created,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.140
like stories, articles, and conversations.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.640
This stops the model from cannibalizing its own earlier outputs.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.680
It keeps answers more accurate and creative.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:42.360
So next time someone talks about model collapse, you can picture it as running out of fresh cookie dough.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.780
You have to add real flour again to save the batch.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.300
And that is basically how model collapse works, not so scary, right?

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.360
Let's move to some cool stuff you can try yourself.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:59.200
First up is a tool called Inter1 that watches video calls or streams in real time.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.320
It picks up on things like engagement, tone, and conversation quality while they happen.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.620
Think of it like a coach watching your game tape and pointing out how the team is working together.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.720
The tool breaks the video into short 8 second windows and sends back updates about how people

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.240
on screen are interacting.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:23.000
This could help with practice interviews, team projects, or learning how to read social cues better.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.300
You do not need to code anything to explore the idea.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:32.580
Go to the Interhuman AI site and check out their demo videos to see the 12 different social signals

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:33.100
it tracks.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:38.100
Then try recording a short 30 second clip of yourself practicing a presentation.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.580
Imagine what signals an AI might notice about your energy and clarity.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.460
Next is something even easier to jump into right now.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:52.300
Some developers are running powerful AI tools entirely inside regular web browsers,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.560
using something called web assembly.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:59.100
This lets code run safely without installing anything heavy on your device.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:03.560
Picture it like setting up a mini science lab right on your kitchen table,

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:05.760
instead of needing a whole school building.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:10.540
You could one day edit videos or test ideas without a powerful computer.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.460
It is still early but the potential for quick private experiments is huge.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:16.940
Try this right now.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.820
Open any modern browser and search for web-based image editor AI demo

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:23.940
or browser AI playground.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:28.200
Pick one that lets you upload a photo and apply effects without signing up.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:33.040
Experiment with turning a selfie into different styles to see how far browser AI

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:34.040
has already come.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.300
Now for a couple of quick bits that caught my eye.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.020
One builder connected an open source AI agent called OpenClaw to physical hardware.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:48.400
This lets the agent actually move and interact with the real world like a simple robot.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.360
The story shows how good AI coding tools are making it easier for regular people to experiment

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.160
with robotics projects at home.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:01.060
Another creator used video generation tools to make fake driving footage.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:07.060
The footage includes masks that show exactly where the driver is looking and how alert they seem.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:13.180
It is a clever way to create practice data for safety systems without filming real people for hours.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.060
That's it for today.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:19.180
Remember every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:24.980
If something we talked about today made you curious go try it that's literally how learning works.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.620
Stay curious, keep experimenting and we'll see you tomorrow.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:34.640
And if you'd rather watch then listen find us on YouTube at Enora Network, links in the show notes.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:41.420
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice, editorial selection and analysis are my own.
